1. German Credit Dataset

1.1 Dataset source correction

Initial download (german_credit_data.csv from Kaggle) was identified as a simplified derivative containing only 10 columns, not the 20-attribute dataset named in the approved proposal.
Corrected by fetching the original UCI Statlog (German Credit Data) dataset via the ucimlrepo Python package (fetch_ucirepo(id=144)), confirmed shape (1000, 21) — 20 features + target, matching the approved proposal.
Saved locally as german_credit_uci_original.csv.

1.2 Missingness check

UCI documentation (statlog_german_credit_data.variables) confirms zero missing values for all 20 attributes and the target.
Verified independently with df_german.isnull().sum() — confirmed all zero.
Decision: No imputation required for this dataset.

1.3 Categorical decoding

Raw values were coded (e.g. A11, A34) rather than human-readable. Decoded 13 categorical columns using dictionaries mapped from the official UCI attribute documentation, applied via .replace().
Note: .map() initially failed silently (returned all NaN) due to pandas 3.0.5's nullable StringArray dtype; .replace() was used instead and worked correctly.
Verified post-decode: df_german.isnull().sum() confirmed zero nulls introduced by the decoding step.
Saved final cleaned file as german_credit_uci_original.csv (overwritten with decoded version).
2. Home Credit Default Risk Dataset (application_train.csv)

2.1 Scope decision

Only application_train.csv was used (307,511 rows, 122 columns). Supplementary tables (bureau, bureau_balance, credit_card_balance, installments_payments, POS_CASH_balance, previous_application) were not merged in, to keep scope manageable given the dataset's already-flagged computational risk (Risk 4 in the approved risk register). application_test.csv and sample_submission.csv were excluded — no TARGET column, irrelevant to this study.

2.2 Missingness calculated as percentage of rows

50 columns exceeded 20% missing; 17 columns had missingness below 20%; remaining 55 columns had none.

2.3 Columns dropped (>20% missing, no viable recovery)

48 columns dropped: mostly building/property physical-characteristic fields (e.g. COMMONAREA_AVG/MODE/MEDI, LIVINGAPARTMENTS_*, FLOORSMIN_*, YEARS_BUILD_*, ELEVATORS_*, WALLSMATERIAL_MODE, NONLIVINGAREA_*, etc.), ranging ~48–70% missing. Pattern consistent with an optional supplementary building survey not completed for most applications. No reliable proxy variable was identified to recover a "not applicable" interpretation for this group.
EXT_SOURCE_1 (56.4% missing) also dropped. Unlike the property columns, this is an external credit bureau score — its absence likely reflects genuine data unavailability rather than structural non-applicability. Logged separately as a limitation given its plausible predictive relevance to credit risk.

2.4 Columns retained above 20% threshold, with justified exceptions

OWN_CAR_AGE (66.0% missing): cross-tabulated against FLAG_OWN_CAR. Missingness confirmed Missing At Random — 202,924 of 202,929 missing cases correspond to applicants who do not own a car. Imputed as 0 for non-owners (FLAG_OWN_CAR == 'N'); the 5 remaining genuine gaps (car owners with missing age) imputed using the median age among car owners.
OCCUPATION_TYPE (31.3% missing): cross-tabulated against NAME_INCOME_TYPE. Missingness concentrated among pensioners (55,357 of 55,362 missing) but also present, less explicably, among ~41,000 working/commercial-associate/state-servant applicants — a messier MAR pattern than OWN_CAR_AGE, with no single clean proxy variable. Retained (not dropped) given its plausible relevance to credit risk (occupation relates to income stability); missing values imputed as a new category, "Unknown/Not Applicable", rather than assuming a specific cause.

2.5 Columns below 20% threshold — standard imputation

AMT_REQ_CREDIT_BUREAU_HOUR/DAY/WEEK/MON/QRT/YEAR (13.5% missing each): confirmed via cross-check that all missing rows are missing across all six columns simultaneously (41,519 rows) — consistent with "no credit bureau inquiry history" rather than random data loss. Imputed as 0.
EXT_SOURCE_2 (0.2%), EXT_SOURCE_3 (19.8%): no clear structural explanation identified; imputed with median (per standard rule in the approved Data Quality Assurance Plan).
Remaining low-missingness columns (AMT_ANNUITY, AMT_GOODS_PRICE, CNT_FAM_MEMBERS, OBS_30/60_CNT_SOCIAL_CIRCLE, DEF_30/60_CNT_SOCIAL_CIRCLE, DAYS_LAST_PHONE_CHANGE — all <1% missing): imputed via median (numeric). NAME_TYPE_SUITE: imputed via mode (categorical).

2.6 Result

Final shape: 307,511 rows × 74 columns, 0 missing values.
Saved as application_train_cleaned.csv.
3. LendingClub Dataset (loans_full_schema.csv)

3.1 Dataset verification

Confirmed loans_full_schema is a legitimate, distinct, well-documented dataset (10,000 observations, ~55 variables) rather than a trimmed/simplified derivative — no substitution needed. Retained as originally downloaded.

3.2 Missingness identified

9 columns with missing values, ranging from 0.24% to 85.45%. Remaining 47 columns had no missing values.

3.3 Joint-income fields (perfect structural MAR)

annual_income_joint (85.05%), debt_to_income_joint (85.05%), verification_income_joint (85.45%): cross-tabulated against application_type. Missingness corresponds perfectly (100%) to individual (non-joint) applications — 8,505 of 8,505 missing cases are individual applications; all 1,495 joint applications have complete values. Imputed as 0 (numeric fields) / "Not Applicable" (categorical field), representing genuine structural absence of a co-borrower rather than unknown data.

3.4 Delinquency/inquiry timing fields (MAR, "event never occurred")

months_since_last_delinq (56.58%), months_since_90d_late (77.15%), months_since_last_credit_inquiry (12.71%): cross-tabulated months_since_last_delinq against delinq_2y. 5,646 of 5,658 missing cases (99.8%) correspond to applicants with zero recorded delinquencies in the last 2 years, supporting the interpretation that missingness reflects "no such event has occurred" rather than an unknown/unrecorded value. All three fields imputed with a sentinel value of 999, representing "no event on record / effectively very long ago."
Limitation noted: sentinel-value imputation is a modelling convenience; tree-based models (Random Forest, XGBoost) can generally learn an implicit threshold around this value, but it would distort results if a linear model were ever applied directly to this feature without adjustment.

3.5 Remaining low-missingness columns — standard imputation

emp_title (8.33%): imputed as "Not Disclosed".
emp_length (8.17%): imputed as 0 (plausible for unemployed/undisclosed applicants).
num_accounts_120d_past_due (3.18%), debt_to_income (0.24%): imputed via median.

3.6 Result

Final shape: 10,000 rows × 56 columns, 0 missing values.
Saved as loans_full_schema_cleaned.csv.
Summary across all three datasets

No dataset was handled with a single blanket rule. The approved 20%-missingness threshold from the Data Quality Assurance Plan was applied as the default, but two deliberate exceptions were made in Home Credit (OWN_CAR_AGE, OCCUPATION_TYPE) where cross-tabulation evidence showed the missingness was structural (Missing At Random) rather than a genuine data-quality failure, and where the variable was judged plausibly important to the study's credit-risk objective. In LendingClub, missingness patterns were similarly diagnosed before imputation, distinguishing "structurally not applicable" (joint-income fields) from "event never occurred" (delinquency-timing fields), and sentinel/category-based imputation was used instead of blind median substitution where that distinction mattered. German Credit required no imputation, confirmed both by official documentation and independent verification.

Outstanding preprocessing tasks (not yet done): categorical encoding (one-hot/ordinal), IQR-based outlier capping, and min-max normalisation, across all three datasets.